22.3  SSH tunnels and port forwarding

TL;DR
  - -L: a port on your machine leads to a port reachable from the server. Reach
    a database that only listens on the server's localhost.
  - -R: a port on the server leads back to your machine. Show a local web app to
    someone who can reach the server.
  - -D: a SOCKS proxy; your browser's traffic leaves from the server.

      ssh -N -L 5433:localhost:5432 db       # local 5433 → db's PostgreSQL
      ssh -N -R 8080:localhost:3000 server   # server's 8080 → your 3000
      ssh -N -D 1080 server                  # SOCKS proxy on localhost:1080

On your machine
  TCP       0.0.0.0:22      all interfaces     user root
  TCP       [::]:22         all interfaces     user root
  TCP       127.0.0.1:631   this machine only  user root
  TCP       127.0.0.1:8080  this machine only  supervisor (PID 4100)
  UDP       0.0.0.0:5353    all interfaces     user avahi
  note: the process of a socket owned by another user needs root: sudo ss -tulpn

Pitfalls
  - localhost in -L 5433:localhost:5432 means the server's localhost: the
    address is resolved at the far end.
  - Forwarded ports listen on 127.0.0.1 only, on both sides, unless you give an
    address (and, for -R, the server allows GatewayPorts).
  - -N runs no command, so the session looks frozen: that's the tunnel working.
    Ctrl-C closes it.
  - "bind: Address already in use": something already listens on that local port
    (13.1).

Deep dive: 2 questions. Read them with idk 22.3 --why

Related
  Commands  ssh, ss
  See also  idk 22.2 ~/.ssh/config, jump hosts and host keys
  See also  idk 13.1 Who listens on which port
